WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.870
In this tutorial, we’ll define the phi and
psi angles of an amino acid, use a 3D model

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:12.690
to visualize them, and see how they’re measured.
Finally, we’ll learn how to translate what

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:19.360
we see in the 3D model to a 2D Newman projection,
which might sound familiar if you’ve taken

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.970
an organic chemistry course.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:27.830
In order to carry out the functions that keep
you alive, proteins fold up into very specific

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:34.090
three-dimensional shapes. The peptide sequence
curls and bends into the secondary structural

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:40.070
elements of helices and sheets, and those
can further fold into the tertiary structure

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.730
of a protein.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.879
When crystallographers and structural biologists
are figuring out the structures of these important

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:53.800
macromolecules they place the atoms where
they appear to go in 3D space based on their

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:00.610
structural data. But there can be some ambiguity
in the model, so they need a way to verify

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:06.540
that their structures make sense based on
actual allowable molecular geometries.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:14.439
Enter the Ramachandran plot. In 1963, the
Indian physicist G. N. Ramachandran described

00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:22.359
a standard way to define the geometry of a
given amino acid in a polypeptide chain. Ramachandran’s

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:29.670
team noted that some geometries aren’t possible
due to sterics. In other words, a given amino

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:37.179
acid cannot adopt some conformations in a
polypeptide chain because of unfavorable crowding

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:43.469
with the adjacent residues. And, when the
two backbone angles are plotted, there are

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:49.429
distinct regions for the unique elements of
the secondary structure.

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:55.880
Phi and psi are examples of dihedral angles,
which are, by definition, the angle between

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:02.369
two intersecting planes, shown in yellow in
this image. Notice that the carbonyl and the

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:08.709
adjacent nitrogen are shown with dotted bonds.
Remember that peptide bonds, and amide linkages

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:15.590
in general, have significant contribution
from a resonance structure with double bond

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:23.569
character. So much so that the nitrogen and
carbonyl group are coplanar. This puts six

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:27.819
atoms in a single plane on each side of the
alpha carbon.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:33.489
Though we don’t have free rotation about
amide bonds, we can rotate around each bond

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:39.810
coming from the alpha carbon atom. Let’s
head over to our 3D model to explore this.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:46.599
We’ll start with a tripeptide made up of
three alanine residues, and for good measure,

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:52.349
let’s show the peptide bonds and label the
N-terminus and C-terminus.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:57.200
When we measure phi and psi angles for our
central alanine, we’ll need to look at some

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.780
atoms in the surrounding amino acids. But
we can simplify our structure by removing

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:11.159
atoms that are not needed to view phi or psi
for the central alanine. Those atoms can obscure

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:15.689
other atoms and make the analysis of the angle
a little more difficult.

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:22.590
The phi angle is defined by rotation about
this bond, from the alpha carbon to nitrogen,

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:28.230
which I’ve colored magenta. And the psi
angle is given by the bond from the alpha

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:35.760
carbon to the carbonyl, which I’ve colored
green. In order to understand the angles,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.269
we need to set this up by looking straight
at the alpha carbon directly down the bond

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:46.790
of interest. So, if we’re looking for the
phi angle, we’ll orient the structure like

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:53.209
this. And if we’re considering the psi angle,
we’ll have to orient the structure like

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:54.969
this.

00:03:54.969 --> 00:03:59.989
When we look down the bond for the phi angle,
this nitrogen is going to be hidden behind

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:06.391
the alpha carbon. What we’ll be comparing
is the angle between these two carbonyl carbon

00:04:06.391 --> 00:04:08.620
atoms.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:14.239
And when we look down the bond for our psi
angle, this carbon atom is going to be hidden.

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:21.030
So here, we’ll be comparing the angle between
these two nitrogen atoms. So, essentially,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:26.170
we consider a four atom pattern when looking
at phi and psi.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:32.310
To translate this to our Newman projection,
we can imagine model as a clock. Looking directly

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:37.940
down our bond of interest, we’ll need to
make sure to place the nitrogen or carbonyl

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:44.270
carbon that is attached to the alpha carbon
at noon. If we draw out just the front carbon

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:51.050
of our Newman projection, this group has to
point up. For the phi angle, our hydrogen

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.870
points left and our methyl points right.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.750
When we draw the front carbon atom for psi,
we’re looking at the alpha carbon from the

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:05.350
opposite side, so our hydrogen points right
and our methyl is on the left.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.669
Now, it’s time to consider what’s going
on with our back carbon, and we’ll do this

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:16.660
in detail for just the phi angle to start.
Let’s hide a couple more atoms to make the

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.229
carbonyl easier to see.

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:27.259
Dihedral angles range in value from negative
180 to positive 180 degrees. If the two carbonyl

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:34.830
carbons are eclipsed, the angle is 0. Let’s
start there, and move incrementally to a phi

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:43.449
angle of 180 degrees. Going back to our clock,
as the carbonyl on the back carbon moves from

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:51.750
noon to 6, the phi angles are positive. Let’s
pause at 90 degrees to draw the groups on

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:58.810
the back carbon onto our Newman projection.
The carbon of the carbonyl is sitting at a

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:06.360
90-degree angle, so we can place it here.
And this hydrogen, the only other group on

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:13.820
the back nitrogen atom is on the opposite
side, 180 degrees from the carbonyl. We can

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:17.340
add that in, and we’ve done it.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:23.610
Once we pass 180 degrees, or 6 o’clock if
we’re using our clock analogy, the values

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:30.800
for phi are negative. So, if we’re between
6 and midnight, we have a negative phi angle.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:37.720
Let’s pause at negative 140 degrees and
draw the Newman projection again. This is

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:43.840
a typical phi angle for an amino acid found
in a beta sheet. And let’s check out what

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.110
that looks like from another angle.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:53.939
Moving right along, negative 60 degrees is
a typical phi angle for an alpha helix. Let’s

00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:58.030
show this Newman projection too.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:09.060
Now, let’s focus on the psi angle in our
model. We’ll rotate our structure, and hide

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.400
just a couple more atoms to clearly see the
nitrogen atom that’s bound to the alpha

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:19.919
carbon. We’ll just show two angles for this
model, and the first is a typical psi angle

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:27.360
for an alpha helix, which is negative 50 degrees.
Adding the Newman projection, we can see the

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:37.800
nitrogen on the back carbon is at about 10
o’clock. For an amino acid in an alpha helix,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:44.360
both the phi and psi angles are negative.
We can see this on our Ramachandran plot.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:50.630
And, we’ll notice that for beta sheet, psi
is positive, as we can see from this region

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.750
of the plot.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:59.330
Positive 130 degrees is a typical value for
the beta sheet psi angle. Let’s show that

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:07.710
in our model. And now let’s draw that up
as a Newman projection. Nice.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:12.330
To sum up, let’s break this process down
into three general steps:

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:18.080
Step 1: Look down the bond of interest with
the alpha carbon in front.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:24.010
Step 2: There will be either a nitrogen or
a carbonyl directly attached to the alpha

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:29.949
carbon, depending on whether you’re looking
down the phi or psi bond. Place this group

00:08:29.949 --> 00:08:31.300
at noon.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:38.270
Step 3: If the other nitrogen or carbonyl
is between noon and 6 o clock, the angle is

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:43.950
positive. If it’s between 6 and midnight,
the angle is negative.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:49.540
I hope this tutorial has helped you begin
to make sense of phi and psi angles and Ramachandran

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:55.450
plots. It’s one thing to watch me demonstrate
this, and another to play around with a 3D

00:08:55.450 --> 00:09:00.670
model of your own. So I encourage you to check
out the video description where there’s

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:07.480
a link to an amazing page on the website proteopedia,
where you can explore phi and psi angles for

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:13.020
yourself. And if you find this challenging,
you are not alone. This type of detailed structural

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.830
analysis is difficult at first, but gets easier
with practice. Happy modeling.

